<section id="token-counter"><h2>Replay a token budget</h2><p>Imagine an agent that reads a policy and a code excerpt on each call. Each branch runs three calls. Each call reads 2,000 input tokens and emits 400 output tokens. Of the input, 1,200 tokens qualify as a cache hit in this example.</p>
<div class="experiment-strip"><label for="lanes">Branches that run the full task</label><select id="lanes"><option value="1">1 branch</option><option value="2">2 branches</option><option value="4">4 branches</option><option value="8">8 branches</option></select>
<table><thead><tr><th>Calls</th><th>Input</th><th>Cached part of input</th><th>Output</th><th>Total tokens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr id="token-totals"><td>3</td><td>6,000</td><td>3,600</td><td>1,200</td><td>7,200</td></tr></tbody></table><output id="token-reading" role="status">One short instruction can start three calls and account for 7,200 tokens.</output></div>
<p>This is illustrative arithmetic. It assumes a fixed amount of work per branch. Running the same four branches sequentially changes the schedule, but the arithmetic still counts twelve calls. A cache hit can change billed input cost while the input still appears in usage. Keep nested fields within their parent totals when reading a provider response. <a href="sources.html#R03">[R03]</a> <a href="sources.html#E08">[E08]</a></p>
<p>For a real run, save a request ID, stage, model, tool calls, input, cache detail, output and acceptance result. Deduplicate by request ID across resumed sessions. Record separate model rates and tool fees before you estimate cost. If the client hides token counts, keep the field unknown.</p></section>
